    "text": "Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>S</mi></math>&nbsp;be the mirror image of the point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Q</mi><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>)</mo></math> with respect to the plane <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math>&nbsp;and let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi><mo>(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>&#947;</mi><mo>)</mo></math> be a point of this plane. Then the square of the length of the line segment <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>S</mi><mi>R</mi></math> is"
  },
